-- js-callgraph.rofl — the CLAIMS the call-graph model makes about itself.
-- Rules derive; this file asserts, and every assertion here is answerable by
-- `npm run report` rather than by reading prose.
--
-- Three ledgers, and they are not interchangeable:
--
--   node_kind / handled / ignored / unknown_because   — js-model's matrix,
--       keyed by babel node KIND x layer. Modelling the call graph made this
--       layer touch kinds nobody had declared; they are declared here, and
--       the count of them is the price of the cell.
--   shape_because                                     — keyed by callee SHAPE,
--       which is the dimension the kind matrix cannot express. `member_expression`
--       HAS a rule and still misses seven of its eight shapes; at kind level that
--       cell reads `modelled` and the residue is invisible.
--   checked                                           — evidence, not claim.

-- ===========================================================================
-- KINDS THE CALL GRAPH DRAGGED IN
--
-- MEASURED, not guessed: `kind_undeclared[audit](K)` derives the kinds this
-- layer's rules touch and `node_kind(js, K)` does not declare. Before this
-- block it reported 17; after it, 0. Those 17 are new rows in a matrix that is
-- LAYER-WIDE, so each one also opens an unanswered dataflow cell — a call-graph
-- rule is what put `this_expression` on the dataflow queue.
node_kind(js, identifier).
node_kind(js, member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_call_expression).
node_kind(js, new_expression).
-- a TS cast in the callee position, named by the classifier since 2026-09-04
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).
node_kind(js, this_expression).
node_kind(js, super).
node_kind(js, array_expression).
node_kind(js, object_pattern).
node_kind(js, object_expression).
node_kind(js, object_method).
node_kind(js, object_property).
node_kind(js, function_expression).
node_kind(js, arrow_function_expression).
node_kind(js, class_method).
node_kind(js, conditional_expression).
node_kind(js, sequence_expression).
-- 2026-09-04: the rules read it as a callee object and the VALUE layer now
-- unions its two operands, so it is part of this layer's vocabulary.
-- `unrecorded_vocabulary[audit]` caught it — a kind the rules touch and
-- `node_kind` did not declare — the same run it was first mentioned.
node_kind(js, logical_expression).
node_kind(js, tsnon_null_expression).
-- the PROPERTY of a computed callee is read by the rules too, so its kinds are
-- part of the layer's vocabulary
node_kind(js, string_literal).
node_kind(js, numeric_literal).
node_kind(js, template_literal).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE RULES ACTUALLY DO — one rule id, r_call_edge, because one rule set
-- is what was written and splitting it into invented ids would be the
-- tautology js-model.rofl already paid for.
handled(js, identifier,                callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, decorator, callgraph, r_decorator_call).
handled(js, optional_call_expression,  callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, function_expression,       callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_method,             callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_property,           callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, object_expression,         callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, class_method,              callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, variable_declarator,       callgraph, r_call_edge).

-- `member_expression` is the honest half-truth of this file. Tier 2 resolves
-- `ns.method()` where ns is a local object literal, so a rule exists and the
-- cell is `modelled` — and seven of that kind's eight shapes resolve nothing.
-- The kind matrix has no way to say so; the shape ledger below does.
handled(js, member_expression,         callgraph, r_call_edge).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, WITH A TYPED REASON. js-model's default for a cell
-- nobody touched is already `not_yet`; these are written out anyway, because a
-- default that is never contradicted is indistinguishable from an oversight.
-- CLOSED 2026-09-07 by w_scope_binding, and the cell was open for a REASON
-- rather than by omission: `this` reaches the call graph as a receiver, and a
-- receiver attributed to a function that does not bind `this` resolves the
-- wrong member. `this_host[flow]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl names the nearest
-- enclosing non-arrow function, and entries 8 and 8b answer for a class method
-- and for an object method.
handled(js, this_expression,                 callgraph, r_this_host).
-- CLOSED 2026-09-07 by w_alias_store. An assignment reaches the call graph two
-- ways and only one of them was answered: as a RECEIVER, `(x = o).m()`, which
-- `plain_assign[flow]` has valued since the catch-all split; and as a WRITE,
-- `obj.x = f` followed by `obj.x()`, which nothing modelled. The second is a
-- `member_value[flow]` arm in rules/js-dataflow.rofl and needs no store — the
-- layer is already flow-insensitive for an assignment to an identifier.
handled(js, assignment_expression,           callgraph, r_member_write).
-- THE MODULE BOUNDARY, 2026-09-07 (w_cg_module_boundary). An imported name may
-- be the function the other module exports under that name — a VALUE fact in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl, read here through `may_be_node` like every other
-- receiver, so the call graph gains no mechanism of its own. The export side is
-- the same question read backwards.
handled(js, import_declaration,              callgraph, r_imported_name).
handled(js, import_specifier,                callgraph, r_imported_name).
handled(js, export_named_declaration,        callgraph, r_imported_name).
-- ...and the four forms this corpus could not exercise are now zero. The last
-- of them, `export *`, was answered on 2026-09-07 and is recorded at line 394
-- with the other three, where the kind is declared.
unknown_because(js, super,                   callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04: the constructor edge is modelled, and the kind is split
-- by the refinement column, so a KIND-level reason has no cell to sit on. The
-- residue that remains is `s_new_result` — `(new C())()` — and it takes the
-- default reason like every other unmodelled shape.
unknown_because(js, optional_member_expression, callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, conditional_expression,  callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, sequence_expression,     callgraph, not_yet).
unknown_because(js, tsnon_null_expression,   callgraph, not_yet).
-- MODELLED, AND IT ALREADY WAS. Measured 2026-09-05 in the callee-position
-- boundary sweep: `(f || g)()` resolves to BOTH arms — a correct two-element
-- may-set through the value layer's union rule — while the callee bucket that
-- held the site said `not_yet`. The rule that does it is the wrapper rule that
-- covers the whole syntactic family; nothing new was written for this row.
handled(js, logical_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge).
-- the receiver is a host literal and its methods belong to the runtime, not to
-- any corpus this analyser can be pointed at
unknown_because(js, array_expression,        callgraph, out_of_scope).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE LANGUAGE DECIDES, and the sweep of 2026-09-05 is where these were
-- settled. A verdict of `not_yet` about a site that can NEVER resolve is not
-- caution, it is a queue entry nobody can ever discharge — and telling the two
-- apart takes knowing JavaScript rather than looking at the tree, which is the
-- same lesson `do { } while (c)` taught the control-flow sweep the day before.
-- DECLARED HERE TOO, and the audit is what asked for it: these three carry a
-- verdict from THIS file and their `node_kind` rows live in the dataflow pack,
-- so in the world test/js-callgraph.test.ts builds the claims pointed at cells
-- that do not exist and `orphan_claim[audit]` named all three by name. A file
-- that makes a claim about a kind declares the kind.
node_kind(js, boolean_literal).
node_kind(js, class_declaration).
node_kind(js, return_statement).

ignored(js, boolean_literal,   callgraph, a_a_literal_is_never_callable).
-- ...and `null_literal`, added 2026-09-06 when `catch { return null; }` entered
-- the corpus with the propagation fixtures and `vocabulary_gap[audit]` named it
-- within one run — the second time in two iterations that gate has caught a kind
-- my own fixture introduced, which is the whole reason it counts rather than
-- reporting nothing.
node_kind(js, null_literal).
ignored(js, null_literal,      callgraph, a_a_literal_is_never_callable).
-- ...and the two that are not call sites at all. `return e` carries a value the
-- VALUE layer reads (that is how `f()()` resolves); it transfers control to a
-- caller that is already on the graph. A class is reached by `new C()`, whose
-- cell carries the constructor edge — the class node is the TARGET of an edge,
-- never the site of one.
ignored(js, return_statement,   callgraph, a_not_a_call_site).
ignored(js, class_declaration,  callgraph, a_the_constructor_is_the_target).

-- ...AND ITS EXPRESSION TWIN TAKES THE SAME WAIVER, 2026-09-08
-- (w_class_expression) — and it takes it only because the constructor really
-- IS the target now, which it was not the day before.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER, on the corpus rather than on a probe. Before:
-- `new (class {})().turn()` and `new (class Hoist {})().lift()` came back as
-- unresolved `s_member_on_ident`, `Gantry.rated()` came back unresolved, and no
-- constructor edge existed for any of them — so the waiver `a_the_constructor
-- is the target` would have been a sentence about a target nothing could reach.
-- After the `class_named` arm in rules/js-dataflow.rofl and the anonymous
-- constructor name above, `calls_in` carries all five edges from
-- test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt by name:
--
--     onAnonClassExpr -> turn        onClassExprStatic -> rated
--     onNamedClassExpr -> lift       onClassExprCtor -> Gantry, -> bear
--                                    onExtendsClassExpr -> twice
--
-- `-> Gantry` is the constructor edge and its NAME is the whole of the second
-- rule: the class has no `id`, so without the binding-name arm the edge is
-- derived by `resolves` and then dropped by `calls_in`, which joins `fn_name`
-- on both ends.
node_kind(js, class_expression).
ignored(js, class_expression,   callgraph, a_the_constructor_is_the_target).

-- A LITERAL AS A COMPUTED KEY. `string_literal` and `numeric_literal` are read
-- for their KIND, which is all a shape needs, and their value is on the facts
-- when a later tier wants it.
--
-- `template_literal` USED TO BE THE ONE THAT WAS NOT RECOVERABLE, and the
-- paragraph here said so: TemplateElement.value is `{raw, cooked}` and the
-- contract emitted scalar own properties only, so the chunk's text never
-- became a fact. That was TRUE and it was never measured. Measured 2026-09-08
-- across 223 files and 422 482 nodes, with babel's `extra` scratch declared
-- out: the scalars-only contract excluded exactly ONE property in the whole of
-- JavaScript as babel presents it, and it was this one. A nested object of
-- scalars is a record of scalars and a four-relation contract can carry it, so
-- the scanner flattens one now and `` o[`k`]() `` selects its key through the
-- `selects[flow]` arm that was already standing.
handled(js, string_literal,  callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, numeric_literal, callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, template_literal, callgraph, r_template_key).
-- ...and a PATTERN, 2026-09-08. A destructured name is a callee like any other
-- once the value layer can say what it denotes; no rule was needed here, which
-- is the second time in three iterations that declaring a kind at this layer
-- cost nothing but the row.
handled(js, object_pattern,   callgraph, r_destructure).

-- ...AND THE OTHER FOUR OF THE FAMILY, 2026-09-08. Three of them cost nothing
-- here for the same reason `object_pattern` did — a name is a callee whatever
-- bound it — and every one is measured by an EDGE the oracle confirms:
--
--   array_pattern       useFirstSlot -> chiselled, useSecondSlot -> planed,
--                       and the two locals are read in two different functions
--                       precisely so the index is observable in the edge SET.
--   assignment_pattern  shaped -> squared (the default) and shaped -> cubed
--                       (an argument passed at the same position).
--   rest_element        useObjectRest -> spanner, and NOT the key the pattern
--                       took: shapes.ts asks for it and the model is silent.
--   spread_element      useObjectSpread -> spanner through an object spread,
--                       and pair -> chiselled / pair -> planed through a spread
--                       ARGUMENT — which is the one that needed a rule, because
--                       `arg_at` had been pairing tree index with parameter
--                       index across a spread and derived `pair -> cubed`, an
--                       edge the running fixture contradicts.
node_kind(js, array_pattern).
node_kind(js, assignment_pattern).
node_kind(js, rest_element).
node_kind(js, spread_element).
handled(js, array_pattern,      callgraph, r_destructure_index).
handled(js, assignment_pattern, callgraph, r_param_default).
handled(js, rest_element,       callgraph, r_object_rest).
handled(js, spread_element,     callgraph, r_spread).

-- ===========================================================================
-- CONTROL TRANSFERS THAT ARE NOT CALL EXPRESSIONS.
--
-- This is the frontier the callee-shape taxonomy cannot see, because it starts
-- from CallExpression and these are not one. Each of them runs a function the
-- call graph never records, and the execution oracle proves it: `new Box(1)`
-- put `useClass -> Box` on the runtime's list and nowhere on the model's.
-- Declared here so the gap is a queryable row rather than a paragraph.
node_kind(js, tagged_template_expression).
node_kind(js, await_expression).
node_kind(js, for_of_statement).
node_kind(js, decorator).
node_kind(js, class_accessor_property).

-- ...AND TWO OF THE FOUR ARE ANSWERED, 2026-09-07. `` tag`x` `` is a TRANSFER
-- SITE, the same shape `new C()` took, and the tag resolves through the value
-- layer like any other callee. A `for-of` is the same shape again and then one
-- step further: it is ONE node and TWO calls, `E[Symbol.iterator]()` and then
-- `next()` on what that returned, and V8 attributes both to the enclosing
-- function — measured before the rule was written, which is what made it
-- checkable at all.
--
-- THE OTHER TWO STAY, and the reason each stays is measured rather than
-- argued: an `await` on a thenable is invisible to the ORACLE — V8 attributes
-- the `.then` call to the promise machinery, so a model deriving it would be
-- contradicted by the instrument rather than confirmed — and a decorator
-- produces no node at all with the parser plugin off.
--
-- WHAT `handled` CLAIMS HERE, precisely: the KIND participates in this layer
-- and a named rule answers for it. Two of the corpus's four for-of sites still
-- resolve to nothing, and they are not residue of the same class — iterating
-- an array literal or a generator calls a BUILT-IN `[Symbol.iterator]`, a
-- function that is not in the program, so there is no node any rule could name.
-- A kind-level cell cannot express that split; the precedent is
-- `member_expression`, handled since tier 2 and still missing seven of its
-- eight shapes.
--
-- ...AND THE SENTENCE ABOVE PUTS TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS IN ONE CLAUSE, measured
-- 2026-09-09 by `hidden_call_*` in rules/js-controlflow.rofl, the census
-- `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` built for its own four positions and pointed at
-- this door too. The two unresolved for-of sites are NOT the same residue:
--
--   for (const chosen of [alef, bet])   an array_expression -> BUILTIN. The
--                                       sentence is exactly right, and it is a
--                                       POSITIVE statement: nothing user-written
--                                       runs, so no edge is missing.
--   for (const drawn of pick())         a generator object, which `may_be_node`
--                                       does not carry -> UNTRACED. And a
--                                       generator's `next` runs the generator
--                                       BODY, which IS a node in this program,
--                                       so an edge may well be missing here.
--
-- One of the two is answered and one is a frontier, and the clause reads them
-- as one because a cell cannot hold the split. `hidden_call_untraced[flow]` is
-- where the second one now sits; the first is `hidden_call_builtin[flow]`.
--
-- THE VERDICT IS LEFT AS IT STANDS AND THE QUESTION IS OWNED BY NAME.
-- `w_the_generator_is_not_a_builtin` in facts/worklist.rofl carries the
-- measurement and claims no cell: whether `for_of_statement/none/callgraph`
-- should reopen on the strength of it is a verdict, and a verdict is the
-- owner's. What is NOT left as prose is the split itself, which is now two
-- rows anybody can query.
handled(js, tagged_template_expression,     callgraph, r_tag_call).
handled(js, for_of_statement,               callgraph, r_iterator_protocol).
-- ...AND THE AWAIT LEFT THIS LIST ON 2026-09-08, and it left because the note
-- above is WRONG about the callee and wrong about the instrument. It reads "V8
-- attributes the `.then` call to the promise machinery, so a model deriving it
-- would be contradicted by the instrument". Two probes, both re-runnable:
--
--   * With `Promise.prototype.then` monkey-patched and a positive control that
--     the patch is live, `await <native promise>` calls it ZERO times on V8
--     11.3 and V8 13.6. The specification's `Await` uses PerformPromiseThen, an
--     internal operation. There is no library method in this construct at all,
--     so the cell never belonged to `w_env_api_surface`.
--   * A USER thenable's own `then` IS called, exactly once, on a stack of DEPTH
--     ONE. The oracle does not contradict that edge — it DROPS it: `runOracle`
--     keeps a frame only when the CALLER's file is a run file, and a caller-less
--     frame carries `file: ""`. Run on a real thenable, the instrument records
--     `<top> -> settle file="" line=0` and the harness keeps nothing.
--
-- So the model says what it can see and refuses the column it cannot:
-- `performed_call[code]` names the callee and NO CALLER, `await_no_call[code]`
-- says an await performs no call at all, and `await_value_unknown[audit]` is
-- the residue. `unverified[audit]` will carry this claim, correctly — the
-- execution oracle is structurally unable to check it, which is a different
-- statement from nobody having tried.
handled(js, await_expression,               callgraph, r_await_performs_then).
-- `decorator` LEFT THIS LIST ON 2026-09-08 with a rule, not a waiver:
-- `r_decorator_call` in rules/js-callgraph.rofl reads the `expression` child
-- through the value layer, and ONE ARM REACHES BOTH SHAPES — `@decoOnce` names
-- the function and `@decoFactory('m')` names a call whose RESULT is applied.
-- Measured: `resolves` gains exactly two rows and the edge set gains exactly
-- `top -> decoOnce` and `top -> decoApplied`.
-- ...AND `class_accessor_property` LEFT IT ON 2026-09-08 THE WAY A FIELD DID,
-- with a ROW rather than a rule: `class_field_kind(class_accessor_property)` in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl puts `accessor sling = slung` into `field_of`, and a
-- member holding a function is a callee target — `this.sling(n)` resolves for
-- exactly the reason `this.strike(n)` already did.
--
-- ITS EXCUSE WAS NEVER A RULE'S ABSENCE. Measured over the corpus that had held
-- `accessor slot = 4` since decorators landed: `member_value` carried no key
-- for it, because there was a DECLARATION AND NO READ and the site could not
-- decide anything. The fixture came first.
handled(js, class_accessor_property,        callgraph, r_field_value).

-- ...AND THE TWO LITERALS WHOSE METHODS ARE IN A LIBRARY, re-typed the same
-- day and for the same reason as the two shape cells in facts/js-shapes.rofl:
-- `1n.toString(16)` and `/x/.test(s)` are attributed by `lib_call[code]` and
-- have no node to reach. `no_source_target` is the model`s own atom for that
-- and it is IRREDUCIBLE, so these leave the work queue rather than sitting in
-- it as if a rule were still owed.
unknown_because(js, big_int_literal, callgraph, no_source_target).
unknown_because(js, reg_exp_literal, callgraph, no_source_target).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE FRONTIER, BY SHAPE. Every shape whose sites do not all resolve has a row
-- here; `shape_unexplained[audit]` is the gate that says so, and it must be
-- empty. `runtime_dependent` appears EXACTLY ONCE, and that is the point of
-- the taxonomy: a computed callee `o[k]()` with a non-literal k names a method
-- that does not exist until the program runs, and nothing else on this list
-- can borrow that excuse. `a.b.c()` is merely unfinished.
shape_because(js, s_computed_dynamic_key,  callgraph, runtime_dependent).

-- ours, and on the queue
shape_because(js, s_identifier,            callgraph, not_yet).
-- FOUR REASONS RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND THE AUDIT IS WHAT DEMANDED IT.
-- `s_member_on_ident`, `s_member_on_member`, `s_member_on_this` and
-- `s_optional_member` used to say `not_yet` and every site of all four now
-- resolves through `denotes` — so `shape_stale[audit]`, the check for an excuse
-- outliving its cause, named them by name the moment tier 4 landed. Deleting
-- them is not optimism: an excuse for a shape whose sites all resolve is
-- decoration, and if a corpus ever brings back a receiver the entries do not
-- reach — an import, a parameter, a host global — the residue returns and
-- `shape_unexplained[audit]` demands a reason again. The verdict follows the
-- corpus rather than the grammar HERE, which is the opposite of the shape
-- vocabulary above, and the two audits are what keep the difference honest.
-- the residue is an AMBIENT receiver: `bag['fixed']` where `bag` is a
-- `declare const` with no initialiser, so there is no value in the program to
-- find. REVERTED TO not_yet 2026-09-04: I had written out_of_scope, and scope
-- is the owner's word rather than mine. Reading a `.d.ts` is undone, not
-- excluded, until somebody with the authority says otherwise.
shape_because(js, s_computed_literal_key,  callgraph, not_yet).

-- `s_non_null` IS TICKED AND STILL HAS RESIDUE, which is what this table is
-- for and `unknown_because` is not: the cell is modelled — `notNull!(n)`
-- resolves through the value layer — while `maybe!(n)` does not, because
-- `maybe` is an AMBIENT declaration with no value in the program. REVERTED TO
-- not_yet for the same reason as above: an ambient declaration's type is
-- readable in principle, so this is undone rather than excluded, and excluding
-- it is not my call. Note the division of labour the two tables have:
-- `shape_because` describes the RESIDUE OF A SHAPE and may stand beside a
-- tick; `unknown_because` describes an UNMODELLED CELL and may not.
shape_because(js, s_non_null,              callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-08, and `shape_stale[audit]` is what named it: every site of
-- `s_computed_template_key` resolves now, so the excuse outlived its cause. It
-- read `knowable at parse time, and absent from the fact vocabulary`, which was
-- true of the contract and not of the language.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04 with the value core: `mkBox(n).peek()` resolves through
-- the callee's RETURN, so this shape has no residue left and `shape_stale`
-- said so. It was the cell the queue recorded as waiting on the dataflow layer.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04: `super.m()` resolves — `super` inside a class method
-- denotes the SUPERCLASS, which is one relation over the class's `super_class`
-- child. `shape_stale[audit]` named this row the moment the rule landed, for
-- the fourth time this session.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-04, AND NEITHER NEEDED A RULE. `new C().m()` and
-- `({a(){}}).a()` were `not_yet` because the corpus had no site for them, and
-- whether the rules covered them was a guess. Two sites in shapes.ts turned the
-- guess into a row: both resolve through `may_be_node` exactly as predicted —
-- a fresh instance reaches its class, an object literal is its own value.
-- `shape_stale[audit]` then named both. The excuse was about the CORPUS and
-- read as if it were about the MODEL, which is the difference a site settles
-- and an argument cannot.
-- `"str".toUpperCase()` and the like: the receiver is a primitive and the
-- method lives on its PROTOTYPE. REVERTED TO not_yet 2026-09-04. I had matched
-- it to `s_member_on_array`'s out_of_scope, and that was overreach twice over:
-- scope is the owner's word, and `s_member_on_array` carries a verdict I did
-- not write and cannot confirm either. Whether this model ever describes
-- built-in prototypes is a real question with a real answer; `scope_unowned`
-- keeps it on the table until somebody with the authority answers it.
-- RE-TYPED 2026-09-08 WITH ITS TWIN. `f_two_reason_relations_over_one_fact_have_now_bitten_twice`
-- records that `shape_because` (the FRONTIER's reason) and `unknown_because`
-- (the MATRIX's reason for the same cell) are taken for one fact by every
-- reader and derive from each other nowhere. Both are moved here in the same
-- edit, on purpose, so the pair does not diverge a third time.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_literal,     callgraph, no_source_target).
-- TWO NEW RESIDUES from the 2026-09-04 split of the object-position catch-all,
-- and each is owned by a DIFFERENT item, which is why they are two rows and
-- not one bucket: `(await p).m()` waits on the control-form work and
-- `` `s`.m() `` answers through the String prototype.
-- `s_member_on_await` RETIRED 2026-09-04: `await` is transparent to the value
-- layer now, the site resolves, and a reason with no residue is what
-- `shape_stale[audit]` exists to catch.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_template,    callgraph, no_source_target).
-- `s_member_on_other` KEEPS ITS ROW even though the matrix now waives the
-- cell, and the distinction is the one this file's header draws: the frontier
-- reason describes the RESIDUE OF A SHAPE, and the catcher has a residue the
-- moment anything lands in it. `unresolved_shape[audit]` reads this relation,
-- not the matrix, so removing the row would make an occupied catcher silent
-- exactly when it matters.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_other,       callgraph, not_yet).
-- RETIRED 2026-09-05 by measurement: `(new Maker())()` where the constructor
-- RETURNS a function resolves to that function, through the same wrapper rule.
-- The shape had carried `not_yet` since it was named and no site had ever been
-- built that could contradict it — the corpus had no constructor with a return.
handled(js, new_expression, s_new_result, callgraph, r_call_edge).
-- MODELLED 2026-09-04 with a residue that is not ours. `super()` reaches the
-- nearest ancestor's explicit constructor — measured against V8, which reports
-- `Cask -> Barrel` through a constructor-less `Keg`, skipping the synthesised
-- frame — and the site that stays is one whose whole chain has no explicit
-- constructor at all. There is no node for the function it calls.
shape_because(js, s_super,                 callgraph, no_source_target).
-- UNREACHABLE UNDER THIS PARSER, waived rather than left as backlog and
-- measured rather than assumed: `s_dynamic_import` is the shape of a callee
-- whose KIND is `import` (babel's `Import` node), and scanners/js_ast.ts parses
-- with `plugins: ['typescript']`, under which `import(p)` arrives as a single
-- `import_expression` and never as a call whose callee is `Import`. Over the
-- whole corpus `callee_kind` takes ten values and `import` is not among them,
-- so no corpus this scanner produces can fill the cell. The CONSTRUCT is not
-- unmodelled — `import_expression x callgraph` carries `runtime_dependent` at
-- kind level, which is the honest verdict about which module a computed
-- specifier loads. This is `s_parenthesized` again: `absent` means unreachable
-- here, not undone, and only one of the two is closable.

-- the receiver is a host literal — see array_expression above
shape_because(js, s_member_on_array,       callgraph, out_of_scope).

-- A shape nothing produced. It exists so that a grammar form nobody
-- anticipated is a ROW rather than a silence, and the day it appears the
-- verdict is already `not_yet` instead of nothing at all.
shape_because(js, s_unclassified,          callgraph, not_yet).

-- ===========================================================================
-- EVIDENCE. Three fixture files: two executed against a V8 stack-trace oracle,
-- one scanned only, because the grammar shapes it carries cannot be spelled in
-- runnable JS. The fixture count is the executed pair — the number the oracle
-- actually ran.
checked(js, call_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge, o_run_with_stack, 2).

-- TWO SHAPES GAINED A RESIDUE ON 2026-09-05, and the residue is the fix rather
-- than a regression. `Vat.poured()` and `new Vat().tapped()` are TypeErrors —
-- an instance method reached through the class, a static reached through an
-- instance — and until the receiver decided which half of a class it can see,
-- both RESOLVED. They now resolve to nothing, which puts one unresolved site
-- into each of these two shapes and `shape_unexplained[audit]` demanded a
-- reason for both.
--
-- THE REASON IS THE ONE ALREADY IN THE TAXONOMY and it fits exactly: the same
-- atom `super()` earned when its whole chain declares no constructor. There is
-- no node for the function these sites name, because the program names a
-- function that does not exist. `not_yet` would be a queue entry nobody can
-- ever discharge, which is the mistake the call-graph sweep spent a day
-- correcting one level up.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_ident, callgraph, no_source_target).
shape_because(js, s_member_on_new,   callgraph, no_source_target).

-- A NINTH RECEIVER SHAPE, 2026-09-08 (w_meta_property). `import.meta.resolve()`
-- is a call into the HOST, and the residue is irreducible for a reason that is
-- in TypeScript's own files rather than in an argument: `interface ImportMeta`
-- in `lib.es5.d.ts` is EMPTY, and `url`/`resolve` are declared in
-- `lib.dom.d.ts` and `@types/node`. `releaseOf` in scanners/ts_lib.ts returns
-- null for both — no release in the file name — so `lib_member` cannot carry
-- them and `lib_unsupported[audit]` could never date them. This is the first
-- residue on this list whose callee is the HOST's rather than the LANGUAGE's,
-- and the distinction is exactly the one the era axis cannot represent.
shape_because(js, s_member_on_meta,  callgraph, no_source_target).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FOUR KINDS THE FRAME ADDED, 2026-09-06 (w_vocabulary_frame). The kind is
-- re-declared here because a file that makes a claim declares the kind — the
-- convention w_vocabulary_home exists to question, and the one in force.
node_kind(js, binary_expression).
node_kind(js, unary_expression).
node_kind(js, class_body).
node_kind(js, template_element).

ignored(js, binary_expression, callgraph, a_a_primitive_is_never_a_callee).
ignored(js, unary_expression, callgraph, a_a_primitive_is_never_a_callee).
ignored(js, class_body, callgraph, a_a_statement_is_not_a_callee).
ignored(js, template_element, callgraph, a_a_primitive_is_never_a_callee).

-- TEN TYPE NODES, 2026-09-07 (w_type_surface). A node in TYPE POSITION is
-- erased before anything runs, so it is never a callee and never a receiver.
-- Declared here rather than deferred: what a type node means at THIS layer is
-- a fact about the language, not the prototype question the deferral named.
node_kind(js, tsfunction_type).
node_kind(js, tsnull_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsnumber_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsstring_keyword).
node_kind(js, tstype_alias_declaration).
node_kind(js, tstype_annotation).
node_kind(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation).
node_kind(js, tstype_query).
node_kind(js, tstype_reference).
node_kind(js, tsunion_type).

ignored(js, tsfunction_type,                callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnull_keyword,                 callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnumber_keyword,               callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsstring_keyword,               callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_alias_declaration,       callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_annotation,              callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation, callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_query,                   callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_reference,               callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsunion_type,                   callgraph, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).

-- DECLARED HERE AND ABSENT FROM THE CORPUS, with the reason written down;
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands one, and a row belongs where its kind is
-- declared.
-- `decorator`'s EXCUSE WENT ON 2026-09-08: the corpus has two, in
-- test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt, and `kind_absent_stale[audit]` named the
-- row within one run of the fixture landing.
-- ...AND `class_accessor_property` NEVER NEEDED ONE. The first draft wrote it
-- an excuse in the same edit that gave it a site, and `kind_absent_stale[audit]`
-- reported the row within one run — an excuse and its cause landing together
-- is the same defect as an excuse outliving its cause, caught by the same rule.
-- `tagged_template_expression` stood here until 2026-09-07 and the corpus grew
-- three sites — two runnable and one in shapes.ts that exists so the `fn_node`
-- guard has something to bite on. `kind_absent_stale[audit]` is what would have
-- said so if the author had not.

-- ...and the two kinds those verdicts are about are declared HERE too, which
-- `orphan_claim[audit]` demanded within one run: a world loading this pack
-- and not facts/js-modules.rofl carried a claim about a cell it had no kind
-- for. Third time this convention has bitten in one session — a row belongs
-- in the pack that declares its subject, and a verdict declares the kind.
node_kind(js, import_specifier).
node_kind(js, export_named_declaration).

-- ...and the four unexercised import/export forms, declared here because their
-- `unknown_because` rows are here. FOURTH time in one session that a verdict
-- written without its kind produced an `orphan_claim` — the convention is
-- that the pack carrying a verdict declares BOTH the kind and the layer, and
-- writing it down here is cheaper than rediscovering it a fifth time.
node_kind(js, import_default_specifier).
-- ...and the attribute, 2026-09-09. Declared in this pack as well as in
-- facts/js-modules.rofl for the reason `import_specifier` is: a pack that
-- CLAIMS a kind must also declare it, or a world loading this pack without
-- the modules one carries a claim with no cell under it and
-- `orphan_claim[audit]` says so. Measured: it did, in test/js-model.test.ts,
-- the moment the claim went in without this line.
node_kind(js, import_attribute).
-- ...AND THE EXCUSE THAT GOES WITH IT. The kind is declared and the SHARED
-- corpus has no site: `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands one or a reason, and
-- this is the reason. It is deliberately the WEAK excuse and not one of the
-- "babel emits no such node" kind, because the construct is emitted — the
-- era corpus has two sites in test/fixtures/js-env/era-attributes.js and the
-- rules in rules/js-modules.rofl section 8 fire there. What is missing is a
-- SHARED-corpus site, and adding one moves node counts, kind censuses and
-- cost pins across files this change has no business touching.
-- EXPECT THIS ROW TO BE RETIRED the day a shared fixture imports JSON, and
-- `kind_absent_stale[audit]` is what will say so — the same retirement this
-- pack already records for `export_all_declaration`.
kind_absent_ok(import_attribute, a_no_corpus_site).
node_kind(js, import_namespace_specifier).
node_kind(js, export_default_declaration).
node_kind(js, export_all_declaration).
-- ALL FOUR HAVE A SITE NOW. `kind_absent_ok(export_all_declaration, ...)` stood
-- here until 2026-09-07 and `kind_absent_stale[audit]` is what takes it away:
-- gamma.mjs is two `export *` lines, so the kind that had no corpus site has
-- two, and an excuse that outlives its reason is a lie the matrix can catch.

-- THE OTHER TWO SPECIFIERS AND THE DEFAULT EXPORT, 2026-09-07. A namespace
-- import binds the module object — the other file's `program` node — and a
-- default import binds the one unnamed export. `export default` had a node
-- and NO CONSUMER until alpha.mjs got one that beta.mjs imports; a form the
-- harness calls is not a form the model can be checked on.
handled(js, import_namespace_specifier, callgraph, r_imported_name).
handled(js, import_default_specifier,   callgraph, r_imported_name).
ignored(js, import_attribute,            callgraph, a_not_a_call_site).
handled(js, export_default_declaration, callgraph, r_imported_name).
-- ...AND THE RE-EXPORT, 2026-09-07, under its own rule name because it is its
-- own rule: `export * from './x'` makes x's NAMES this module's, and the arm
-- recurses on `exports_name` so a chain of them works at any depth. The site
-- is gamma.mjs, which declares no function and re-exports two modules, one of
-- which nothing else imports.
handled(js, export_all_declaration,     callgraph, r_reexported_name).

-- THE TWO EXPORT FORMS WITH NO NODE IN THE VOCABULARY, 2026-09-08
-- (w_export_specifier_forms). `export { a as b }` is where a module's EXTERNAL
-- name for a binding stops being its internal one, and `export * as ns from` is
-- where a module offers ANOTHER module under a name of its own. Both are read in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl and both reach this layer through `may_be_node` like
-- every other value — the fourth time in this loop the module boundary has cost
-- a value fact and no mechanism at all.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE THE RULE: with `export { hidden as shown }` beside `export
-- function plain`, `exports_name` derived exactly one row — `plain` — and the
-- cross-file call `usesShown -> hidden` was missing with its site reported as an
-- unresolved `s_identifier`. SILENT, not wrong-named.
node_kind(js, export_specifier).
node_kind(js, export_namespace_specifier).
handled(js, export_specifier,           callgraph, r_export_rename).
handled(js, export_namespace_specifier, callgraph, r_namespace_reexport).


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THREE VALUE FORMS THE VOCABULARY NEVER NAMED, 2026-09-08
-- (w_update_and_literals). ONE of the three cells is answered here and TWO ARE
-- LEFT OPEN with an owner, which is the honest split rather than the tidy one.
node_kind(js, update_expression).
node_kind(js, big_int_literal).
node_kind(js, reg_exp_literal).

-- AN UPDATE EVALUATES TO A NUMBER OR A BIGINT, always, whatever its operand
-- was: the result of ToNumeric plus at most one. There is no operand for which
-- `i++` denotes a function, so no call site can resolve through one. Same
-- verdict and same reason as `unary_expression` beside it, and the fixture
-- gives it the site that verdict never had.
ignored(js, update_expression, callgraph, a_a_primitive_is_never_a_callee).

-- THE OTHER TWO CELLS ARE NOT WAIVED AND NOT ANSWERED, and the reason is the
-- one `w_prototype_of_a_value` already recorded about `array_expression`:
-- naming the residue is not resolving it. `/x[0-9]/.test(s)`,
-- `MATCHER.test(s)` and `255n.toString(16)` are member calls whose METHOD IS
-- NOT A NODE IN THIS PROGRAM, so no rule here can produce an edge — every one
-- of them lives in a standard library this model does not have.
--
-- WHAT CHANGED IS THAT THEY ARE NAMED. Measured before the two
-- `kind_prototype` rows landed: all three were `unresolved_call` with
-- `prototype_of` EMPTY, so `stdlib_member[audit]` — the relation that exists
-- to collect exactly this residue — could not see them. It names all three
-- now, which moves them from silence into `w_env_api_surface`'s in-tray, and
-- the two claims in facts/worklist.rofl are re-pointed at that item by name.
-- An `ignored` here would have been a blocker wearing a waiver's face; see
-- f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands for the distinction.
-- ==========================================================================
-- ES2022 CLASS SYNTAX, 2026-09-08 — queue item w_class_fields. The kinds are
-- re-declared here because a file that makes a claim declares the kind.
node_kind(js, class_property).
node_kind(js, class_private_property).
node_kind(js, class_private_method).
node_kind(js, private_name).
node_kind(js, static_block).

-- A FIELD IS A CALLEE TARGET when it holds a function, which is how a bound
-- handler is written in modern JavaScript: `strike = (n) => struck(n)` and then
-- `this.strike(n)`. MEASURED BEFORE THE RULE — with the fixture in and nothing
-- written, both calls in `Coin.value` came back as `unresolved_call ...
-- s_member_on_this`, because `member_value` reached only `class_method`.
--
-- AND THE NAME IS MEASURED AGAINST A FRAME rather than assumed: V8 reports
-- `getFunctionName() === "strike"` for the arrow a field holds, so `fn_name`
-- can be compared against an execution oracle.
handled(js, class_property,         callgraph, r_field_value).

-- A PRIVATE MEMBER RESOLVES LEXICALLY. `this.#rim(n)` and `this.#mark(n)` both
-- resolve — the method directly, the field through the function it holds — and
-- neither goes through `member_value`, `selects` or the receiver's value at all.
--
-- ONE HALF OF THE CLAIM IS SHORT AND IT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE
-- FOUND: `resolves[code]` reaches a private method and `calls_named` CANNOT
-- SPELL IT. V8 names the frame `#rim`; the store holds `rim`, on the identifier
-- under the `private_name`; the two namespaces are disjoint (`rim` and `#rim`
-- are both members of the fixture's class) so the bare name would conflate two
-- functions, and the kernel has no operation that BUILDS a string — `str_pre`,
-- `str_seg`, `str_segs`, `str_sub`, `str_char` all cut one up. Recorded as
-- f_a_private_method_resolves_and_cannot_be_named.
handled(js, class_private_property, callgraph, r_private_binding).
handled(js, class_private_method,   callgraph, r_call_edge).
handled(js, private_name,           callgraph, r_private_binding).

-- A STATIC BLOCK IS NOT A CALLEE and needs no rule to be one. MEASURED, not
-- assumed: a call inside the fixture's static block is attributed by the
-- ordinary `top_call` arm to the file root, because a `static_block` is not a
-- `fn_node` and nothing encloses the site — and that attribution is CORRECT for
-- a top-level class declaration, since running the shape shows the static block
-- executes at module evaluation, before any instance exists.
--
-- WHAT THE ATTRIBUTION CANNOT SAY, stated because it is a real loss: V8 names
-- the frame `<static_initializer>` (and an instance field's
-- `<instance_members_initializer>`), so the model's caller and the runtime's
-- caller are different names for the same moment. That is why this fixture is
-- in the SCANNED half of the corpus — see f_a_class_initialiser_is_a_frame_with
-- _no_function.
ignored(js, static_block,           callgraph, a_a_statement_is_not_a_callee).
